{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.049,
            0.861,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Proportional Reasoning–2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.089,
            0.628,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "We can extend this to ratios with multiple terms."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.108,
            0.846,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "Let us look at the earlier example of making a concrete mixture. We need to mix cement, sand, and gravel in the ratio of 1 : 1.5 : 3 to get the concrete."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.156,
            0.845,
            0.213
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Example 3: For some construction, 110 units of concrete are needed. How many units of cement, sand, and gravel are needed if these are to be mixed in the ratio 1 : 1.5 : 3?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.217,
            0.847,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "For 1 unit of cement, we need to add 1.5 units of sand and 3 units of gravel. Together, they add up to 5.5 units of concrete. We need to do this 20 times $(110 \\div 5.5 = 20)$ to get 110 units of concrete. So each term has to be multiplied by 20."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.291,
            0.651,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { c } { { 1 \\times 2 0 = 2 0 \\; \\mathrm { u n i t s \\; o f \\; c e m e n t } , } } \\\\ { { 1 . 5 \\times 2 0 = 3 0 \\; \\mathrm { u n i t s \\; o f \\; s a n d , \\; a n d } , } } \\\\ { { 3 \\times 2 0 = 6 0 \\; \\mathrm { u n i t s \\; o f \\; g r a v e l } . } } \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.353,
            0.769,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.602,
            0.845,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "So, we need 20 units of cement, 30 units of sand, and 60 units of gravel to make the concrete."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.639,
            0.845,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "When we divide a quantity x in the ratio $a : b : c : \\dots ,$ the terms in the ratio are —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.684,
            0.797,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "$\\begin{aligned}\\overline{X} \\times \\frac{a}{(a + b + c + \\cdots)}, \\overline{X} \\times \\frac{b}{(a + b + c + \\cdots)}, \\overline{X} \\times \\frac{c}{(a + b + c + \\cdots)}\\end{aligned}$ , and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.722,
            0.846,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 4:** You get a particular shade of purple paint by mixing red, blue, and white paint in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5. If you need 50 ml of purple paint, how many ml of red, blue, and white paint will you mix together?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.292,
            0.785,
            0.705,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Red}\\;\\mathrm{point}=50\\times\\frac{2}{(2+3+5)}=50\\times\\frac{2}{10}=10\\;\\mathrm{ml}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.289,
            0.815,
            0.708,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Blue\\ paint} = 50 \\times \\frac{3}{(2 + 3 + 5)} = 50 \\times \\frac{3}{10} = 15\\ \\mathrm{m}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.283,
            0.843,
            0.715,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{While\\ point}=50\\times\\frac{5}{(2+3+5)}=50\\times\\frac{5}{10}=25\\mathrm{~ml}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.874,
